  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#creating-a-new-browsing-context states that the origin of document is set to be the creator origin, if there is a creator browsing context.\r\n\r\nHowever, it is unclear if this origin should be aliased or cloned.\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<body>\r\n<div id=\"log\"></div>\r\n<script>\r\nfunction debugLog(s) {\r\n  var log = document.querySelector('#log');\r\n  log.appendChild(document.createTextNode(s));\r\n  log.appendChild(document.createElement('br'));  \r\n}\r\ndocument.body.appendChild(document.createElement('iframe'));\r\ndocument.domain = window.location.hostname;\r\ntry {\r\n  window[0].document;\r\n  debugLog('Same origin!');\r\n} catch (e) {\r\n  debugLog('Cross origin!');\r\n}\r\n</script>\r\n</body>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis snippet produces:\r\nChrome: Same-origin!\r\nEdge: Cross-origin!\r\nFirefox: Same-origin!\r\nSafari: Same-origin!\r\n\r\nThough 3 browsers currently say 'same-origin', I'd like to make the spec explicit here that it clones, not aliases. It seems pretty strange for `document.domain` to potentially affect multiple browsing contexts.",
